//// Decision 21's T2 on the METHOD axis — a method call whose receiver carries
//// no type inference could place. Policy 3 puts a method in its TYPE's module
//// on erlang, so the call needs an owner module, and the owner came from the
//// method's name alone when nothing else declared it. The name only identifies
//// a type when nothing else declares it — and "nothing else" is every type the
//// PROGRAM declares, not the ones this file happens to import.
////
//// Here `main` imports both `toArray`s and the receiver of each call is a
//// generic return from another module, which inference leaves untyped. The
//// imported index was keyed by the method NAME alone, first writer wins, with
//// no dissent check, so the export index's hash iteration order picked one of
//// the two owners for BOTH calls, and both defect shapes are on the page:
//// `Grouping`'s body over a `Query` reads past the tuple and the program dies
//// with `{error, badarg}` (which is how it was measured on this branch, on the
//// first line), while `Query`'s body over a `Grouping` reads the neighbouring
//// `key` and answers `1` where commonJS answers `2`, with nothing said about
//// it. Both are the one defect, and which one runs is not a choice this
//// program makes.
////
//// The local collision below is the control: `putMethodOwner` has always keyed
//// `method_owners` by `name/arity` and cleared the entry when a second type
//// claimed it, so the name never decided on its own inside one module.
////
//// wasm is not in `targets`: the wasm backend stays single-module.

pub mod shapes;
import {queryOf, groupOf} from "shapes";

pub type Bag<T>(
    items: Array<T>,
) {
    pub fn unwrap(self: Self) -> Array<T> {
        return self.items;
    }
}

pub type Bucket<K, V>(
    key: K,
    items: Array<V>,
) {
    pub fn unwrap(self: Self) -> Array<V> {
        return self.items;
    }
}

pub fn bagOf<T>(xs: Array<T>) -> Bag<T> {
    return Bag(items: xs);
}

pub fn main() {
    @print(queryOf([1, 2, 3]).toArray().length);
    @print(groupOf("k", [1, 2]).toArray().length);
    @print(bagOf([1, 2, 3, 4]).unwrap().length);
}
